(& Wanderweg finally)
- I walked under a chestnut tree that was dropping
everything yesterday onto the road and it reminded me
of an uphill bit I did out of Brandenburg on (probs)
the 66-lakes-trail a while back. That day felt
brutal; it was the longest walk I'd done in ages
despite only being 27km-ish. I think that's
the thing with planning this trip - I've gambled on
my body getting with the program with the / by the
Wednesday hike. And gambled with the weather,
I guess ☺

- Beech trees are fun to walk under because the
fourthree-pointed seed pods make a really satisfying
crunch sound, when they're empty.

- Should look up sloes vs. Damsons.
Sloes: always Thorns (except hybrids)
10-15mm fruits
black/blue, often with a bloom
Damsons: 2-3cm fruits, slightly oval
dark blue/purple → these are just... Zwetschgen
lol.
I think I've just gotten it.

It's now almost 8am. I've had the window open all
morning in my tiny cupboard-under-the-stairs room
(except, it's in the corner of a building).

The tourists are all gone and so this morning along comes
a street sweeper and a man with a leaf-blower to blow
all the seeds and crap into the paintbrush of the sweeper.
Making an absolute racket. At the same time, someone
comes through to change all the bins.
Someone told me yesterday that there are 3000 inhabitants
and 300 thousand visitors per year. This year, I have been
one.
Turns out the UK govt runs a "check for flooding" service
where you can see the river level stats live. 8-24cm
is a normal depth, flooding is from 40cm, and "highest
ever" is 60cm, in 2007.

## 19:10
Looking back through the photos - _so_ many nice moments
today. But first, I'll write down some stories:

- I ran into a number of people walking from Moreton
in Marsh - some Australians who did a round trip from
Longborough.
Moreton in Marsh to... Blockley. Met them because
we were all trying to go to the pub and it absolutely
wasn't happening (seemed to be spontaneously shut - in the
words of a local "Mondays are always risky"). Also
a bunch of older ladies, who were walking Moreton-in-
Marsh to Chipping Campden. I... actually rounded a
corner while one of them was peeing (but far enough
away that they ~~do~~ didn't notice) and then held
back for a plausibly deniable amount of time.
